     },
     "text": "Let \\(B_ r(x)\\) denote the closed disc of centre x and radius r and let m be plane Lebesgue measure. The authors prove the following Theorem: Let \\(F\\subset R^ 2\\) be a Lebesgue measurable set with \n\\[\n \\limsup_{r\\to \\infty}r^{-2}m(F\\cap B_ r(0))>0. \n\\]\n Then there is a positive number \\(r_ 0\\) such that for every \\(r>r_ 0\\) there exist x,y\\(\\in F\\) with \\(| x-y| =r.\\) \n\n\nThis theorem was conjectured by Sz\u00e9kely and a very complicated proof based on the ergodic theorem was found by Weiss. The authors give a more simple proof.",
     "contribution_type": "review"
    }
   ],
